<h2 id="methodology">Methodology and what was measured</h2>
<p>Every advertiser here was queried in the <strong>Meta Ad Library</strong>, the public database of currently running ads, by page ID rather than brand name. That distinction matters: two of the three advertise under a page name that differs from the brand a buyer would search for.</p>
<div class="meth">
<p><strong>Creative measurement.</strong> Video files were downloaded from the hosted ad assets. Duration, resolution, aspect ratio and audio presence come from <code>ffprobe</code>. Hard cut counts come from ffmpeg scene detection at a 0.35 threshold. Average shot length is duration divided by shot count. Frames were sampled at one per second to read beat structure and on-screen text.</p>
<p><strong>Creative age</strong> is decoded from metadata on the hosted file and reflects when the asset was created, which can predate the ad going live. It is the only available proxy for whether an ad is working.</p>
<p><strong>Funnel measurement.</strong> Each storefront's public product feed supplied the price ladder. Pages were loaded, left to settle, then measured for load time, DOM node count, request count, and which trust elements appear.</p>
<p><strong>What cannot be measured.</strong> Meta publishes spend and impression data for political and issue ads only. For commercial ads it publishes nothing. No figure in this benchmark represents anyone's performance or budget, and any inference from creative longevity is labelled as inference.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="mechanics">The mechanics: length, cuts, shot rate, captions</h2>
<p>Nineteen videos were measured. The headline finding is that <strong>two editing rhythms dominate and almost nothing sits between them</strong>.</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Creative type</th><th>Typical length</th><th>Avg shot length</th><th>Reads as</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Brand-produced product film</strong></td><td class="n">21 to 30s</td><td class="n">1.9 to 2.2s</td><td>Fast, dense, one claim per cut</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Creator-fronted monologue</strong></td><td class="n">27 to 51s</td><td class="n">4.6 to 4.9s</td><td>Conversational, lets a face hold frame</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Long-form response film</strong></td><td class="n">56s</td><td class="n">4.3s</td><td>Structured argument, objection to proof</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Single-take demo</strong></td><td class="n">24s</td><td class="n">no cuts</td><td>Silent, saveable, reads as organic</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Mixing those two rhythms inside one ad is the most common editing error in the set. A creator talking to camera cut at two seconds reads as frantic. A product film cut at five seconds reads as slow.</p>

<h3>Caption coverage is the clearest quality divide</h3>
<p>One advertiser captions effectively <strong>100% of runtime on every film</strong>, using five distinct caption systems matched to voice: two-line uppercase with a coloured active word, a highlight-box style, thin sentence-case subtitles, outlined bold, and a brand-clean style with a persistent logo watermark.</p>
<p>At the other end, <strong>three ads carry no on-screen text at all</strong>. One of those runs 37 seconds with 16 hard cuts and, played muted, communicates nothing beyond a person exercising. Every claim that ad makes lives only in the caption above it, which is collapsed behind a "see more" link in feed.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>The reusable rule.</strong> If an ad's argument does not survive with the sound off, it does not have an argument. Roughly one caption per beat, and one claim per caption, is what the disciplined advertisers here actually do.</p></div>

<h3>Aspect ratio follows placement, not preference</h3>
<p>The disciplined pattern is <strong>9:16 for creator content and Reels, 4:5 for feed-first brand assets</strong>. One advertiser ships every asset at 9:16 including feed placements. One ships a 3:4 product film. Vertical and near-square together cover the overwhelming majority of paid social delivery, so a landscape master recut with bars is the worst of the available options and is read as low quality by the platform.</p>

<h2 id="hooks">Hook taxonomy: what is saturated, what is open</h2>
<p>Twenty-six openings were mapped against six hook types. The distribution is lopsided.</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Hook type</th><th>Count</th><th>Example opening</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Offer-led with a number</strong></td><td class="n">8</td><td>"Before you spend $200 on private lessons&hellip;"</td><td>Saturated</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Contrarian claim</strong></td><td class="n">6</td><td>"Not bands. Real cable mechanics."</td><td>Saturated</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Confession</strong></td><td class="n">4</td><td>"I couldn't stand working out until&hellip;"</td><td>Contested</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct address</strong></td><td class="n">4</td><td>"Tired of fighting your strength bands"</td><td>Contested</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Diagnostic question</strong></td><td class="n">3</td><td>"If your swing feels stiff, don't just hit more balls."</td><td>Underused</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Pattern interrupt</strong></td><td class="n">1</td><td>A dog at a fence, five seconds before the product</td><td>Wide open</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Twenty-six hooks, and exactly one opens on something that is not fitness content. Every other ad announces itself as a fitness ad inside the first second, which means they are all competing for attention that has already been granted. The single pattern-interrupt ad was also the newest asset in the set at ten days old, which suggests at least one advertiser has worked this out recently.</p>
<p>The diagnostic question is the second opening. Three uses across twenty-six, two of them seasonal or sport-specific. For a product category whose entire premise is that something in the buyer's training is not working, that is a strange thing to leave on the table.</p>

<h2 id="proof">Proof architecture: what earns belief</h2>
<p>Seven proof types appear across the category: institutional adoption, athlete endorsement, customer volume, review score, awards, origin story and named testimonial. What separates advertisers is how many they stack and whether the proof survives the click.</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Proof stacked</th><th>Survives the click?</th><th>Reviews on the hero product page</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Six types</strong> including institutional adoption, pro-team endorsement, US manufacturing and a licensed clinician on camera</td><td>Repeated verbatim on site</td><td class="n">Not audited</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Five types</strong> including customer volume, origin story and three whitelisted creators</td><td>Yes, fully</td><td class="n">908 at 4.87</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Four types</strong> including a television origin and a 45,000 customer claim</td><td>Fails on reviews</td><td class="n">5 at 4.0</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>One type</strong>, a 100,000 customer claim</td><td>No</td><td class="n">None rendering</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>The minimum credible bar.</strong> Based on where this category clusters, the floor is <strong>three proof types, one of which must be third party, and one of which must survive the click</strong>. In practice that means a review score with real volume behind it, one external validator such as institutional adoption or a credentialed voice, and a hard specification. A four-point-zero average on five reviews is a weaker signal than showing no rating at all.</p></div>
<p>One further note on reading proof: the highest-proof advertiser in this set sells between $1,149 and $7,210 including a facility bundle. Institutional adoption at that price is earned by selling to businesses, which is not a route available to a consumer product at a fifth of the price. Proof has to be calibrated to tier.</p>

<h2 id="shipping">Shipping terms across the category</h2>
<p>Shipping is the most common reason a cart is abandoned and the one offer lever every advertiser here treats differently.</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Terms</th><th>What it is doing</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Free, geographically scoped</strong></td><td>Free to the contiguous states, fee elsewhere by order value</td><td>Simplest trust signal. One badge, no arithmetic for the buyer.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Threshold plus weight tiers</strong></td><td>Free above $99, $15 flat below, heavy goods charged separately from $25 to $135 by cart weight</td><td>The threshold lifts average order value while the weight tiers stop bulky items eating the margin it gives away.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Bundle incentive only</strong></td><td>Free shipping attached to specific bundles, not sitewide</td><td>Uses shipping as a reason to trade up rather than a reason to buy.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Charges, both directions</strong></td><td>No free shipping, and the customer pays return postage</td><td>Weakest position in the category, and it compounds an absent guarantee.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2 id="continuity">Does the landing page keep the ad's promise?</h2>
<p>Every claim from a live ad was checked against the page it points to. The best performer held <strong>five of seven claims</strong>, with its central five-word positioning line appearing identically in the ad copy, burned into the video for six unbroken seconds, and as the first feature on the product page. That is claim discipline, and it is the most copyable thing in this benchmark.</p>
<p>The worst performer broke on multiple claims at once. A live ad headline promised up to 30% off while the site banner and every catalogue price showed 15%. A film promised training "at home" while the footage was shot entirely in a public park. A 100,000 customer claim sat above a product page whose seven review containers all rendered empty.</p>
<h3>Errors any operator can check for in an hour</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dynamic product ads serving raw template code.</strong> Two advertisers in this set are running live ad headlines containing an unrendered <code>&#123;&#123;product.name&#125;&#125;</code> token, one of them since December.</li>
<li><strong>Catalogue feeds with no curated product set.</strong> One advertiser's catalogue ads promote a $25 hat and a backordered case rather than the flagship product.</li>
<li><strong>Review widgets installed but empty.</strong> Seven review containers present in the page, all rendering nothing.</li>
<li><strong>Specifications that disagree across live ads.</strong> One product is described as 2.5 lb in three simultaneously running ads, 2.85 lb on the website, and "just under 3 lbs" on screen in a fourth.</li>
<li><strong>Mobile zoom disabled.</strong> One advertiser pins maximum viewport scale, blocking pinch-zoom, which is an accessibility failure on the page paid traffic lands on.</li>
<li><strong>Seasonal copy running out of season.</strong> A January new-year hook still live in August, and a "travel week" ad still running more than two years after the week in question.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="takeaways">What operators should take from this</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pick a rhythm and hold it.</strong> 1.9 to 2.2 seconds per shot for a product film, 4.6 to 4.9 for a person talking. Do not average the two.</li>
<li><strong>Caption everything.</strong> If the argument dies with the sound off, there is no argument.</li>
<li><strong>Ship 9:16 and 4:5, natively framed.</strong> Reframe per shot rather than cropping a landscape master, and keep text clear of the top 15% and bottom 20% where the interface sits.</li>
<li><strong>Open somewhere unexpected.</strong> Pattern interrupt and the diagnostic question are close to unoccupied in this category.</li>
<li><strong>Say one sentence everywhere.</strong> The strongest account here repeats five words in the ad, the video and the product page without variation.</li>
<li><strong>Clear the proof bar before scaling spend.</strong> Three proof types, one third party, one that survives the click.</li>
<li><strong>Audit your own claims the way you would audit a competitor's.</strong> Every defect listed above was found in under an hour with public tools, and every one of them belongs to a well-funded brand.</li>
</ul>

